[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19367e3-97ce-5783-bf46-3e1a0dadc9a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235616 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235305 :multistep/context "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235732} :multistep/id #uuid "cbb272f1-f5dc-5a8e-a5ad-90bddee2754a" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f134fa4c-64bb-58bd-84ad-1201fc21df9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234927} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4e456a-4483-5eff-ad5e-b8059b52670d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24759de2-6f72-5171-8480-c17060b3ce38" :question/math-academy-id "q-132976" :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{6}{125}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "01dc8d52-2ae7-5911-afc7-c49662b4b596" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19367e3-97ce-5783-bf46-3e1a0dadc9a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29686a44-42f0-5647-a0ea-c233a9f3a573"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc58bfdf-5c61-564b-9179-b99d997e008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd67718-6c2e-5e11-a160-7539ce5a4737" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76551198-3b30-5f90-a773-8f46f7e7ea21" :question/math-academy-id "q-132980" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4bc77df3-20f6-580a-a04a-5887403aae86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8185b83-eb29-58df-bdeb-f269a4c2146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{3}+3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829} :answer-field/id #uuid "719e6a79-1ce1-54b1-8c76-98d46b85de7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "632a77f1-9681-5dcd-aa11-3a8387a03565" :question/math-academy-id "q-132978" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{\\left(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f748901-34d8-5fe5-b396-65da16b93764" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a468e96-25a5-56bd-8c4e-55aa7609caeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234732} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a6ec18-b647-50d7-bc61-8f33928303b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9106fff9-5741-53cb-af97-18e37fb205d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-132979" :question/problem "The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=1$ is given by ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12}{2+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d88e23b-5c31-5656-b3b0-9b92982733e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0422cb62-0c61-58da-94f7-f0c7e04bc54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{ln}(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234557} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27d93e9-cbea-537e-8fd4-f771273be507" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7449abb3-586b-5fd3-93e0-df3f3d5afa01" :question/math-academy-id "q-132982" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2{t}^{2}+3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ab744994-87f7-5099-bac2-49706fc2ba63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d80e90-19de-5aa6-bcad-6a5bb1a4fd17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b721cbfc-ca6a-5758-b736-d995eaa6a3ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "369639c0-e92c-5d4b-ae74-ad350d77cc3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234635} :answer-field/id #uuid "32afc963-90c6-5ddd-84bf-c6f9d36217ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a15160-ab8a-5bf1-89d7-e7a6938ca472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234821} :answer-field/id #uuid "973c8ac4-663e-56fb-92f2-63696685ebee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3472053-ac41-53b0-9388-a29832276fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234903} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff8a7b0-8332-5974-8a12-bf5ec591e1f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0042828e-27b2-590d-a108-55b85ccb3e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-132981" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)}=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)=2{t}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $t(2{t}^{2}+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n6-12{t}^{2}=(At+B)t+C(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 6 & =3C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(2)(2(1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A+B+10 \\\\ A+B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(2)(2(-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A-B+10 \\\\ A-B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=-16, \\\\ A-B &=-16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-16$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} & =\\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c98220d8-639c-5178-adae-bfba15a28166" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76e91d9-d765-5281-a065-4b2b27aecc3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234909} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc224230-70b1-516b-b826-e5d3b2c72595" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ba61357-83e7-534d-8757-d228a1221f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-132977" :question/problem "It can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e14a647f-6504-5a8a-8c4e-9ea21ec34f96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2ffda4-7650-5f8b-b0fe-b79f26714743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234619} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3d02e6-9dc2-5595-90e5-be19d90fb6ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "295b1f08-4feb-5011-b27e-88e7956a72df" :question/math-academy-id "q-132975" :question/problem "${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef53cf9f-fb49-5488-8597-598e2df1105f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235586}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391} :step/id #uuid "29686a44-42f0-5647-a0ea-c233a9f3a573"}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e6b660-e7fa-5392-b0f6-a50bdfd47004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ba49d8-2db6-5100-8d54-7ea280ac61ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "221f7f46-e501-5c4b-9474-541896c7450d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167635" :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=3,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235636 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235301 :multistep/context "Consider the functions $f(x)={x}^{B}+Ax,g(x)=B{e}^{x},$ and $h(x)=sin⁡x+1,$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants to be found." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235659} :multistep/id #uuid "8fd21532-89f6-5c25-a765-13883174e1e0" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5979fbe9-034d-5191-82a4-e0ccdb34ea12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234684} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c8f9ae-1e5a-5b02-b529-bd2c894e7f02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bffa0056-7e7b-560f-b52d-460b92da84c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167632" :question/problem "If $A$ is the maximum value of $h(x)=sin⁡x+1$ then $A=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n* Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n* Then, shift it by $1$ units **up**.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/db/dbe1701a42a160e982bff96b1085727080c39b87ca2c99886e3e153e2f5964cc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=sin⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\nsin⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $1$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡x+1\\le 1+1 \\\\ sin⁡x+1\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $sin⁡x+1$ is $A=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "06023932-8236-551e-8944-bf02e1c2d973" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e6b660-e7fa-5392-b0f6-a50bdfd47004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ba49d8-2db6-5100-8d54-7ea280ac61ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "221f7f46-e501-5c4b-9474-541896c7450d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167635" :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=3,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3df2343a-bbb7-5b34-aa08-c03d63c3a1bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7467eff-3284-5ff8-926d-e47188223d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234908} :answer-field/id #uuid "75fbad98-0a41-5480-ba06-edccdf81f980" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "413988df-903b-557d-87b6-8594e3b605b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167636" :question/problem "If $k(x)=g(x)-f(x),$ where $A=2$ and $B=3,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 3 and 4, respectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =g(x)-f(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-({x}^{3}+2x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the addition and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45bb2f18-2e9e-5b44-8e6d-835ab02f773e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6608a0-c52d-5197-a2c4-db2292e6e723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234595} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ba6e34-7aaf-5bfb-b233-f307218d28f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8cb5259c-7ead-550f-9414-cb453c189596" :question/math-academy-id "q-167633" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e1d2c8fef007596dc6fd9b6ef0813fafeb7014490ff35d993f276a2a96ccb13.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            If the graph above is generated by the function $y=sin⁡(Bx),$ then $B=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $3$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/54/544997dc11f7c88d5aa7b3f78d8dd1059a0ac08092726983897c5992d03acf8b.png)\n\n \n\nWe know that for the function $y=sin⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5913bc92-a4b7-5aae-8c83-91eeba377481" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd74cf7-7832-5d4d-9bdb-4352f71d9f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944} :answer-field/id #uuid "6614a1f6-a87a-5480-bfb4-b31cd6dddad3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c736f2-25bd-5d43-acf7-389cd352b94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650} :answer-field/id #uuid "86dd5a39-356d-58f6-a814-f6ba7ef31193" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd151ce5-c877-5cb0-954c-57ccd397f03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb953ef-938a-5b5b-99c5-5fa2a0878578" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80153a7b-80ae-54f5-b4ee-35a981cc0cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740} :answer-field/id #uuid "914f87c0-b0d8-5ddd-be80-6c4d6303ec75" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb796ec5-fb4b-5f50-95bf-f4781e9dc4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed723404-e7d9-5542-afad-7590ee940dcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed1a25d3-8778-5a6a-8c12-425fa1c7957d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167631" :question/problem "Complete the following facts about the function $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$).$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The range of the function is $[$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$].$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The period of the function is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-5}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n![](images/96/96bd60d23237f836b77a26c5c0461a5c2a474554f0a14266b53fc5e7d999f1b7.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n* The domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n* The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n* The period of $sin⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$"} :step/id #uuid "5b1c7505-862b-5fd8-891f-db395428467e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88156c2a-6396-5272-96f1-20a7a5353148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234752} :answer-field/id #uuid "954845d2-9f34-59bb-b74f-2f53b2eea7db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d9641da-73ff-557d-8b86-2f3f56c81c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-167638" :question/problem "The tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at $(c,0).$ Find the value of $c.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$ In step 7, we found that the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $m=1.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the curve at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=k(0)=3{e}^{(0)}-(0)^{3}-2(0)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =(1)(x-0) \\\\ y-3 & =x \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of this tangent line, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ 0 & =x+3 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-3,0),$ and we have $c=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "9858bdd7-c68f-520a-814f-257f6c87fa70"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88ca2c1b-08ce-5224-a752-7911554c06e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234755} :answer-field/id #uuid "616098ac-de92-57f7-a966-f500d556595b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "840e39fc-48e8-52c6-b612-48460181a806" :question/math-academy-id "q-167637" :question/problem "Find $m,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $m=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is equal to ${k}^{′}(0).$ From step 6, the derivative is\n\n$$\n{k}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=0$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0}-3(0)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =3-0-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $1.$"} :step/id #uuid "b4e39b02-a0ee-5216-8f15-fc24fb6ca6c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f42bdd68-d7ff-53db-b3b0-bdc3bed81956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b42b4d1-0b65-5db6-846b-bc92dccc887b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ced32af-bad9-5a14-a058-555b5adb8c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-167634" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc7fb253-09d5-5a21-9268-9ccb9408c367" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235636}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456} :step/id #uuid "3df2343a-bbb7-5b34-aa08-c03d63c3a1bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235643}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425088a0-8b6d-555a-87b1-28f844922b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581142} :answer-field/id #uuid "6867512d-b53b-53db-a5a5-f341bd835d41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8913b80e-2bcb-5364-9080-3c746725b920" :question/math-academy-id "q-167587" :question/problem "Given that $A=3$ and $B=2,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=3$ and $B=2$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581783 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581638 :multistep/context "Consider the functions $f(x)={x}^{A}+Bx,g(x)=A{e}^{x},$ and $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants to be found." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581792} :multistep/id #uuid "c23f7a6e-cbaa-5fb7-aaf2-6238c225b397" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93f73284-0e9b-5c96-b87d-c3745d738fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581289} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fbf00b-1b3a-5580-a4d2-3e128e08f36a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bdc048f4-b72a-50bd-b8d0-4cdfb4fc7ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-167589" :question/problem "Find $m,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is equal to ${k}^{′}(1).$ From step 6, the derivative is\n\n$$\n{k}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=1$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{′}(1) & =3{e}^{1}-3(1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =3e-3-2 \\\\  & =3e-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $3e-5.$"} :step/id #uuid "026db3f0-fcfa-5246-8fc0-1c4b5751f042" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e474f280-0dcb-5a74-9360-80f0b8bc7393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^{x}-3x^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581446}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581446} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0998189-79e2-57fe-b9e4-93993f5aad3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6295d094-28df-5cb3-bdcf-8516048cd5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167588" :question/problem "If $k(x)=g(x)-f(x),$ where $A=3$ and $B=2,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=3$ and $B=2$ in steps 3 and 4, respectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =g(x)-f(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-({x}^{3}+2x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the addition and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cda85cc-99a7-5b70-a2e9-e9f084656cc3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3882936a-f929-5beb-a059-4d094035fe64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581122} :answer-field/id #uuid "0671fc56-8578-5cd8-8388-43460188b159" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd5b3471-5104-54ed-b7e5-116e5c49fb9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167585" :question/problem "![](images/94/94017619f6cc33e722871b43eaeafae66b7a5b676437e2d7566e0327fdfc1547.png)\n\nIf the graph above is generated by the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx),$ then $B=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $2$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\pi .$  \n\n![](images/e6/e6ffe31ddd54026eff7666ecfc0827b7b6e532c65da83a0d34a6ea627a2432f3.png)\n\n \n\nWe know that for the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\pi ,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84b4e24f-fcda-5fa8-a961-da4f5f047d57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f7bc27-1105-5cdd-8f09-e57da9de96e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3e-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2557854d-cac1-5f60-98d5-be2464c24476" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "babd57f4-622e-563f-838e-aab914b121a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167590" :question/problem "The tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=1$ intersects the $x$-axis at $(c,0).$ Find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$ In step 7, we found that the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is $m=3e-5.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the curve at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=k(1)=3{e}^{1}-(1)^{3}-2(1)=3e-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3e-3) & =(3e-5)(x-1) \\\\ y-3e+3 & =(3e-5)x-3e+5 \\\\ y & =(3e-5)x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of this tangent line, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(3e-5)x+2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3e-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5-3e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5-3e},0),$ and we have $c=\\frac{2}{5-3e}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a6db9696-ad2f-5a4c-a7b9-6465f5e5934a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425088a0-8b6d-555a-87b1-28f844922b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581142} :answer-field/id #uuid "6867512d-b53b-53db-a5a5-f341bd835d41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8913b80e-2bcb-5364-9080-3c746725b920" :question/math-academy-id "q-167587" :question/problem "Given that $A=3$ and $B=2,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=3$ and $B=2$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b526b5b6-baff-5720-a6d8-406972200426" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e2a5fc-0c5d-52bb-a9c0-5ab8e77a88a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581027} :answer-field/id #uuid "476dacdf-08b4-54ee-8f1a-0f160dbcf951" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "059639c7-85db-5376-92a1-7c0278857972" :question/math-academy-id "q-167584" :question/problem "If $A$ is the maximum value of $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n* Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n* Then, shift it by $2$ units **up**.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/20/20c3c4c86b54f69f547c9ad64f99f2ef4aaa506a5466f355839fbe0b7761e98d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $2$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\le 1+2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2$ is $A=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "cf6f3cdf-5d7c-5d10-ae90-6b89c1f675f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8cd71ab-47df-5569-b283-26c01213e10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581397} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5c37e9-7096-5259-984d-dfda4c9baeee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a49b6457-0f05-59d9-855e-88b85b73a0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167586" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d9d04f3a-6bfb-5924-ad54-5731a61d71dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234fbd64-3e71-5154-ba26-c33ca9ec6950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581076} :answer-field/id #uuid "32061a8c-5090-50ce-ba79-da5f40f5cbb1" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581529} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3aeab3b-a5ca-5a04-bcdb-bed224c74207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581481} :answer-field/id #uuid "727182ea-b0dc-5650-931f-1144963d2573" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb6337cc-bf42-5a29-9bf5-2dc359f58d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581467} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9965c6-ca41-50a9-81f8-b456e5eca1c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9315993c-5171-5b3f-94b3-42d7c3c23737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581287} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2515abb-3b8d-5ba1-84a6-8154a443be88" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb0a0e-82c0-5586-856c-f6410ae81b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581156} :answer-field/id #uuid "d76c56e7-b4cc-5cbe-a907-4b7404595cc6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bac76f4a-4116-5fca-a94a-36ce5cbe8b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-167583" :question/problem "Complete the following facts about the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$\n\nThe range of the function is $[$\n{{field-3}}\n$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$].$\n\nThe period of the function is\n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/96/96bd60d23237f836b77a26c5c0461a5c2a474554f0a14266b53fc5e7d999f1b7.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n* The domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n* The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n* The period of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$"} :step/id #uuid "eb784615-b954-5b9f-bbdf-0955ea5ad082" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581787}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581701} :step/id #uuid "b526b5b6-baff-5720-a6d8-406972200426" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581750}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db33716-add6-5f35-bf02-1d51985de66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585431} :answer-field/id #uuid "095f41d4-028c-5d7c-986c-c74e9a83226f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "002408ea-9be6-53e5-9b20-0951e4cd4bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-167617" :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=4,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=4$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4.$  \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585864 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585713 :multistep/context "Consider the functions $f(x)=2{x}^{A}-B,g(x)=A{e}^{x},$ and $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1,$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants to be found." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585840} :multistep/id #uuid "65636e3c-ba71-595d-8fca-e2e645b391f9" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4810ba34-77ea-5a34-a27a-3cf916715d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^{x}-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585305} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0c6c1c-c430-5f34-a621-1cbabed95a67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38430f89-356c-59db-b840-c709ecc69408" :question/math-academy-id "q-167618" :question/problem "If $k(x)=g(x)-f(x),$ where $A=2$ and $B=4,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=4$ in steps 3 and 4, respectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =g(x)-f(x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-(2{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the addition and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "042d4a3c-8fce-5faf-8f58-1abf13a41039" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585831}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29461806-df33-5b39-be35-866fd3074fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585256} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae013ee-c1fe-512d-89b1-147997a9efda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5114a99-5b96-5a8d-a93d-d780a272fe96" :question/math-academy-id "q-167619" :question/problem "Find $m,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4.$ \n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is equal to ${k}^{′}(0).$ From step 6, the derivative is\n\n$$\n{k}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{x}-4x.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=0$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{0}-4(0) \\\\  & =2(1)+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "48f7ce6e-e680-5880-91a4-35b54439255c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23337482-f74a-5d1f-b577-860f6a0b4559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585245} :answer-field/id #uuid "83eb2538-2ed3-55ec-a3ee-5eb2903716c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "245b4aa4-34e2-5df8-8b00-7562006b9c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-167615" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3d286778e489326007b655da6a6a168c287a7918f497ef743d7365eaa001d86.png)\n\nIf the graph above is generated by the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx),$ then $B=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $4$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/35/3507eac70c6af174bd0c561054b20af697e5322082769362999c8ab79e10bac2.png)\n\n  \n\nWe know that for the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e60f784-a06e-545c-ad90-99bfdd878205" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caf21e2-fc61-5e24-b6b7-fd1910833d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585312} :answer-field/id #uuid "07179b00-838d-5070-b9cc-b4816f36e3d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f4eb63d-3cd4-5c79-9e7f-dd4bb5c1c5b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167616" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80f76d32-2bf4-5cfa-8487-5f00239f02c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585864}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab5cdca-189e-52b2-be54-a0503ca79375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585397} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ba861f-b15d-52e9-8a84-3a7d609f4655" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585609} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417289e7-c73a-5143-86c0-e7e05ba57000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585299} :answer-field/id #uuid "35efb24a-9e94-5377-b7da-2b0ab09e50f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7dc364-5a72-58cf-815e-4e86750bd04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585260} :answer-field/id #uuid "55aa2ba0-d01d-5f53-89a6-8e83b2a3c1c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af9c512a-03d5-54ac-b2a9-8c61ffa45614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585482} :answer-field/id #uuid "6407632e-5356-5c51-949f-e505f5602aa1" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5cb174-5766-5bd4-93bd-41748015067f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585259} :answer-field/id #uuid "80bbfc2a-a5e7-5f5d-a854-bfe10f2ea1cd" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b74a3d3-c29e-513b-a913-e51860e5cc88" :question/math-academy-id "q-167613" :question/problem "Complete the following facts about the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$\n\nThe range of the function is $[$\n{{field-3}}\n$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$].$\n\nThe period of the function is\n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n![](images/ce/cebd0527c2b8b3bcdf2948da0771103f17412ff058d74b96db1287a7046ba3bc.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n* The domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n* The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n* The period of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$"} :step/id #uuid "838ffef3-334d-5953-8fd3-8548b7ad0679" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3405d49c-ff3f-5b2b-b9d6-ce8c4b63678e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585277} :answer-field/id #uuid "624ba28a-29ac-59c7-81fb-d6083d4d24fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3453fcbe-0459-518e-824e-de5b3f9ef823" :question/math-academy-id "q-167614" :question/problem "If $A$ is the maximum value of $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n* Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n* Then, shift it by $1$ unit **up**.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/63/63c5d512f4fb063567612c549f3d2066460634d581423380bffd415330f63f0d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $1$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1\\le 1+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1$ is $A=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e31372f9-420a-59e8-acd7-8b3b9b7691bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d8628f-5a77-5185-a962-2339fde508b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59c53ed-df2d-5989-a60b-f6ff8c418fc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "194c89ba-2a04-5a19-bba1-eee321fd5899" :question/math-academy-id "q-167620" :question/problem "The tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at $(c,0).$ Find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have $k(x)=2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4.$ In step 7, we found that the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $m=2.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the curve at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=k(0)=2{e}^{0}-2(0)^{2}+4=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(6) & =2(x-0) \\\\ y-6 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of this tangent line, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2x+6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-3,0),$ and we have $c=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e42c1fab-3e86-5520-8b62-542eed0d678a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db33716-add6-5f35-bf02-1d51985de66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585431} :answer-field/id #uuid "095f41d4-028c-5d7c-986c-c74e9a83226f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "002408ea-9be6-53e5-9b20-0951e4cd4bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-167617" :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=4,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=4$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4.$  \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fbf8dd52-f4ce-5cd3-9d19-ca33c44829dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585818}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585714} :step/id #uuid "fbf8dd52-f4ce-5cd3-9d19-ca33c44829dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585818}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf90b86-ed00-521f-866e-457741256953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(t^2+3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3de83bc-b404-5457-8f8e-eb85acb80364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6cc6368e-df43-5cbe-829c-1d64f41be4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132779" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594799 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594664 :multistep/context "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594743} :multistep/id #uuid "cda1fd0e-ac68-5a35-9383-0f5598e0801a" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c14e8a2-231e-520a-9373-a71e0dd19e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t(t^2+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594381} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec6431b2-e799-5371-92fe-40e4255fa0c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "016c901a-a8ed-57f7-ace0-713928705ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132770" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=({t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)={t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}+3)^{′}=2t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20d0338e-6c99-560a-9f28-36ead8792c28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1a1b94-93f5-578c-9ba8-0782dc7efc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-t^2)}{(t^2+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e92d3b6-f81b-5607-9689-c086fed1c87f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43f1bd78-4717-51a3-befe-5422d55c09c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132771" :question/problem "${x}^{′}(t)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=t$ and the denominator $h(t)=({t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(t)\\cdot (4t({t}^{2}+3))}{{(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}-4{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)}{({t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+3)-4{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{t}^{2}+3}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4e825905-2af8-534d-be57-86148ab7ab0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96dafa68-a935-5a06-a7d1-49074e56dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594477} :answer-field/id #uuid "3238cdec-a457-52b9-b1d3-95be6ab04c89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "753ac6b8-07cd-52b3-96d2-729d969d6571" :question/math-academy-id "q-132776" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6t)t({t}^{2}+3)-(3-3{t}^{2})(3{t}^{2}+3)}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}-(9-9{t}^{4})}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}-9}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{4}-18(1)^{2}-9}{{1}^{2}({1}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-18-9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "56f9d5b3-131a-5dab-a77f-a594bb29509e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae7528ec-10b6-55f7-8f1c-53ebf849ca5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594501} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d29de4-4d70-5c3a-943d-158fb07bdc2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3585b6c-38d2-5526-907e-92be04d386c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132775" :question/problem "The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=2$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=2$ second equals\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=2$ is given by ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{3-3(2)^{2}}{2({2}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-12}{2(7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{14}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "637d9130-bf54-5029-bf2f-4661e357d79f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e27fd7f-262e-5ee5-a734-35adede96c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-t^2)}{t(t^2+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594406} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a3f643-3be8-5786-872f-38048e3ae4f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45164ca3-58bc-567c-b0a5-b6ced364360e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132774" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6cb6d9b6-6fa8-500c-aa26-eb513a541877" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c60e67-d7b8-5931-bdbc-a020616746e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594394} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a2452a-e791-5cd5-bd76-96d020cbd13f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb7bf595-1975-527d-836c-bc3f74101723" :question/math-academy-id "q-132773" :question/problem "It can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\nThe particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 3-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=1.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "a23e8b64-aa0c-527c-b649-abac84d05030" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76dc0e60-bb21-52fa-b3b5-624c95fabdc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594446}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594446} :answer-field/id #uuid "203ba5f5-54c2-5c36-a429-a979b4f9e105" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8813aa64-faaa-57ff-9c6e-541105333fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-132772" :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3-3(1)^{2}}{({1}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ab1e8fef-df1a-5eb7-be58-125af0e1290f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756ec7a5-259c-535e-8e4e-8bca0acf467d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\ln(t^2+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594444} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dd2aef7-02b0-5d96-b7d9-689abbb5a8a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a0dab7c7-78c7-5c5a-b26f-c3f00da1a8a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132778" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-4t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,${{field-1}}\n$+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Let $u={t}^{2}+3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-4t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af9e3dc0-60ea-5d3d-a1ca-8efd596fc0f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c8938b-6d96-50d4-9b8d-d5cf5ccba52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594374} :answer-field/id #uuid "5985dee4-185c-5588-a803-81e1531666b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc1dac47-389d-55a2-bd04-e7f99d9c817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594588} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec5fb6b-89f4-59d8-84ba-4907380fe976" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f0a480-4c55-5f01-8833-da5064d409b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594351} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85c2358-2629-5970-b657-ecf0de94761b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ed3f1212-92f0-507b-9a4c-66cb2aca8c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-132777" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,B=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,C=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}=\\frac{At+B}{{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)={t}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $t({t}^{2}+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n3-3{t}^{2}=(At+B)t+C({t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 3 & =3C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(1)({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 0 & =A+B+4 \\\\ A+B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(1)((-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ 0 & =A-B+4 \\\\ A-B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=-4, \\\\ A-B &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-4$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)} & =\\frac{-4t}{{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d04f5e2a-be70-571b-83f3-82a9663f3818" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf90b86-ed00-521f-866e-457741256953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(t^2+3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3de83bc-b404-5457-8f8e-eb85acb80364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6cc6368e-df43-5cbe-829c-1d64f41be4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132779" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcac2414-e8c1-5d28-ad8f-c80378c8a59b"}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690} :step/id #uuid "dcac2414-e8c1-5d28-ad8f-c80378c8a59b"}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0cfd63a-4b5a-533e-a2be-e7bf4a561271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{(t^2+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601770} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0fb97d3-41c9-50ca-834c-975b2638d484" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a588b6ca-ca51-5122-a816-0bdade4e8a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-133003" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-3\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2t}-\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{t})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\right)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602022 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601892 :multistep/context "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{3t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601972} :multistep/id #uuid "a3bb6abd-7c7c-5aed-acb5-3ed09c221aa1" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74072f02-38c0-5024-9927-d20fbb01adbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601683} :answer-field/id #uuid "539664b2-5a32-542d-8175-1e8e8f433e71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601837} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a745778-3183-5c05-8110-8237d9650845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601625} :answer-field/id #uuid "95aa6686-e0ee-5e90-8a08-b11d48ed46da" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "955fba33-db3a-5035-a0f0-a36e69ce29f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601714} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab0261a7-cd72-50ac-977c-9dae25ddbf41" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd74a7a2-cdcc-5ff1-9736-0af3131424f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133001" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,B=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,C=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2t({t}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{At+B}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)={t}^{2}+1$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $2t({t}^{2}+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n1-5{t}^{2}=(At+B)2t+2C({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)2(0)+2C({0}^{2}+1) \\\\ 1 & =2C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)2(1)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)({1}^{2}+1) \\\\ -4 & =2(A+B)+2 \\\\ 2(A+B) & =-6 \\\\ A+B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)2(-1)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\ -4 & =2(A-B)+2 \\\\ 2(A-B) & =-6 \\\\ A-B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=-3 \\\\ A-B &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2t({t}^{2}+1)} & =\\frac{-3t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}-\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "39191ebb-fe2b-5523-a595-65289edb264d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55139606-5928-55a4-b814-22a969ada28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6t(t^2+1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601654} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc71457a-ed2e-5826-8272-b8f37053afd2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c125870a-b46c-59e0-a9be-37ea8a9a1d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-132994" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+1)^{3}]=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=({t}^{2}+1)^{3}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}+1)^{′}=2t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{3})^{′}=3{g}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+1)^{3}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =6t({t}^{2}+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a564dc4-eff1-5aa9-9b50-6ccd895b8292" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43079ce8-151a-5152-a24a-e5da1234ff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601645} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e5df51-7333-55d0-803b-b78a961b4249" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54c981ae-be5a-5bfc-88ce-f58b8abe9462" :question/math-academy-id "q-132997" :question/problem "It can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\nThe particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 3-15{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3 & =15{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "42b1123f-d292-5e13-8aec-86789f6538f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba4971b4-5fc5-5be9-85c1-08df9db2d0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-5t^2}{2t(t^2+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601747} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd9f6dc-6f9c-5153-b277-b67a8607c889" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80fa6f19-761f-5d90-8c95-8ca0436d63bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132998" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{3t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{3t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}{6t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-5{t}^{2})}{6t({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2t({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48f802f5-2dec-542b-b39b-b8c76a191ea2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba64c69b-cc61-5c5a-91cb-3584d2e56959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\ln(t^2+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601749} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1f54e24-e84c-5110-b234-dcdc01d00ee5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a98d0d6-1583-59a6-ae32-af730e3d9590" :question/math-academy-id "q-133002" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-3t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,${{field-1}}\n$+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Let $u={t}^{2}+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-3t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a158fd12-45cd-503b-9f4e-e9c8b873188c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92808948-137e-5c85-80f3-ca6872b617ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-5t^2)}{(t^2+1)^4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601710} :answer-field/id #uuid "0283afc9-033b-510d-8c92-08c4b2e7028d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b0577da-7ab4-5f35-8240-a0665db66cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132995" :question/problem "${x}^{′}(t)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{3t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=3t$ and the denominator $h(t)=({t}^{2}+1)^{3}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=3,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=6t({t}^{2}+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)^{3}-(3t)\\cdot (6t({t}^{2}+1)^{2})}{{(({t}^{2}+1)^{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)^{3}-18{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}[3({t}^{2}+1)-18{t}^{2}]}{({t}^{2}+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+3-18{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a42d100c-909d-5628-b51f-1e963f8a6cae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a25aa99-ad85-52c5-a751-deb8bfaaa95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601704} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca40dc0-3dbf-52b7-a4f7-8c563d57ea20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37ec79f1-7de8-5687-881e-8c807b4797da" :question/math-academy-id "q-133000" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+2t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10t)(2{t}^{3}+2t)-(1-5{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+2)}{(2{t}^{3}+2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20{t}^{4}-20{t}^{2}-6{t}^{2}-2+30{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{t}^{4}-16{t}^{2}-2}{(2{t}^{3}+2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(5{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-1)}{4({t}^{3}+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-1}{2({t}^{3}+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{5(1)^{4}-8(1)^{2}-1}{2({1}^{3}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c2ab1b57-eff3-52c4-9917-72cbfa2d0088" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0cfd63a-4b5a-533e-a2be-e7bf4a561271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{(t^2+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601770} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0fb97d3-41c9-50ca-834c-975b2638d484" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a588b6ca-ca51-5122-a816-0bdade4e8a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-133003" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-3\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2t}-\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{t})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\right)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ddbc6ff2-6380-5dd8-9f4e-cdefba050ea5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5ef275e-f964-5b12-af33-ae7599caf1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601764} :answer-field/id #uuid "78609a79-7e64-5975-b775-9aee8e4b9248" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abeee208-4b09-5c16-9f86-38ef8bebdf35" :question/math-academy-id "q-132996" :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3-15{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3-15(1)^{2}}{({1}^{2}+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-15}{{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e57d6f85-27af-5627-839d-377d9f6bf256" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cace5281-3fbe-5432-b8d2-9a5c18f56b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601767} :answer-field/id #uuid "414d9141-0dcc-5430-ad0e-77c473ba57d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a7343ed5-2fb0-55c8-ae8a-2f27efb70c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-132999" :question/problem "The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=1$ is given by ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1-5{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+2t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1-5(1)^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea389613-7716-5540-879d-9eccac89d302" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602014}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601926} :step/id #uuid "ddbc6ff2-6380-5dd8-9f4e-cdefba050ea5"}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541193 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541053 :multistep/context "An eagle flies along the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5}),\\,y=y(t),\\,t>0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters relative to its nest at the origin, and $t$ is measured in seconds. The functions ${x}^{′}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}(t)$ give the eagle's horizontal and vertical velocity, respectively." :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541207} :multistep/id #uuid "b3bf487d-f311-547b-902e-6194ab2bcbfe" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88892d02-3412-5492-af0a-49ebe61d9b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540755} :answer-field/id #uuid "70887428-4f7c-590f-a390-58a8f2bf8ee3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "143c70c1-44db-5544-97bc-c449974fa03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133292" :question/problem "The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Calculate this displacement.\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "In part 7, we found that the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\n$$\n\nBy the fundamental theorem of calculus, we take the antiderivative and evaluate the difference at the bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =y(t){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t)|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}-2(6))-(\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-2(0)) \\\\  & =60-0 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is $60\\,\\text{m}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0b6a8ccd-d70c-5f07-bd06-c1a67bb8c918" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725} :answer-field/id #uuid "796d2edf-72cd-5edb-a250-8f0040849fed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08e75bed-6305-557a-ac6f-4a738a0fec12" :question/math-academy-id "q-133287" :question/problem "The eagle's path traces a curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane. Suppose that the eagle is at the point $P$ on $C$ at time $t.$ Expressed in terms of $t,$ what function gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P?$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $C$ at time $t$ equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to $C$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{\\left(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e869152-61a2-5f4c-b677-eb10be0bff89" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541225}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5800f9f-a331-5cd2-b8d9-dac2799c0d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "742d438b-e3dc-52b0-a068-888e70c6be15" :question/math-academy-id "q-133290" :question/problem "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$y\\left(\\frac{110}{23}\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y\\left(\\frac{110}{23}\\right)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{110}{23}\\right) & ≈L\\left(\\frac{110}{23}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23\\left(-\\frac{5}{23}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25ed461a-1ae1-5bcc-a645-a1adcac020ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f220aba-10fa-5140-91ce-bb3c746dbdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4a5e43e-e7a6-55b0-a709-f89ec3a86933" :question/math-academy-id "q-133293" :question/problem "Let $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ be the position vector of the eagle. Find the distance between the eagle and its nest at time $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe eagle is approximately$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$ away from its nest after $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Since the nest is at the origin, the distance between the bird and its nest at time $t=5$ is $|r(5)|.$\n\nWe know that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3}.$ Evaluating the function at $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right),\\frac{95}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{95}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(5)| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{95}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9\\,025}{9}} \\\\  & ≈31.68\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "39c190e2-19cb-57ce-9a89-1a038703f741" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541218}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18a0d99-6ac2-55bc-b565-e5db3275b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540851} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a95aab-3ec7-5d58-8faa-efd0ff8b9e68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95631ad4-7573-5741-9c54-4f854208188c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133286" :question/problem "The vertical velocity of the eagle increases at a rate proportional to the square of the time $t.$ In addition, the eagle loses $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ in the positive $y$-direction due to wind resistance. It is known that the eagle's vertical velocity equals $2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe differential equation that describes the eagle's vertical velocity is ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The vertical velocity increases proportionally with ${t}^{2}$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=k{t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality.\n\nSince ${y}^{′}(2)=2,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =2 \\\\ k(2)^{2}-2 & =2 \\\\ 4k & =4 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5cd9358c-320d-5663-953d-407f8219b7ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "780c444c-203b-507b-affb-974182c4ecb1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16e017f-d9d9-54ce-97a3-a549b27da1ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e20ee7e-741b-5069-a51c-7de25cc32da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133285" :question/problem "Calculate the horizontal velocity of the eagle as a function of time.\n\nThe horizontal velocity of the bird is$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by ${x}^{′}(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(t/5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}/25}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by the function ${x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9e4e6674-5e6f-501b-bec3-9bdb487d5059" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541186}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c621d975-7e42-532e-8cd1-a5a95ff3741c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540860} :answer-field/id #uuid "280a3379-43b0-5e7e-835c-37f28ffa4d1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdc34de8-c927-5f9c-8dbf-b5874f7e2681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540873} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d783132-2979-54b3-9fd1-5a7588deaa6d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1baae25-37ff-504a-9b40-31c45e15f352" :question/math-academy-id "q-133294" :question/problem "${r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\,$$⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n* For the $x$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{5}{25+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n* For the $y$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-2=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}),{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{6},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c41b1b6f-713f-5c06-8fe9-5de055592653"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfa30b5-acb3-5f10-b07c-946951bce63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540736} :answer-field/id #uuid "7164cda6-7d07-5c84-a4dc-5817c12576d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e2f173-a72c-520b-adec-a73efbc497dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540853} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5bc5e64-6160-5d46-8ae4-099d090712d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb009f1b-54cb-513a-ae27-a2c22d910e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133289" :question/problem "The eagle is descending for all $t$ in the interval$\\,t\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "If the eagle is descending, then its vertical velocity is negative.\n\nWe know that the eagle's vertical velocity is given by ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nTo calculate the time interval in which the eagle's vertical velocity is decreasing, we solve the inequality ${y}^{′}(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2 & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & <2 \\\\ |t| & <\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $t>0.$ Therefore, the eagle is descending for $t\\in (0,\\sqrt{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "e6e7506e-0ef6-58fd-a0a5-b890fb1061ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541225 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b291071b-8c33-51cd-93d8-3d6f97e2bee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540826} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12141e2-6a38-52ff-846b-6b7417e1ae0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4086d61d-9c18-5ba7-b425-e5509f913d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-133288" :question/problem "The tangent line to the eagle's flight path is horizontal at $t=${{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{2}\\notin (0,\\infty ),$ so we only consider the value $t=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{5}{27}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the curve's tangent is horizontal when $t=\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "eb5ce766-c910-5578-addb-d9c3fe513f7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541223}}]} :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108} :step/id #uuid "780c444c-203b-507b-affb-974182c4ecb1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541158}}]}]
